==Solution 2 (Smart way when running out of time)==
Another way you can approach this is by solving it as if we don't care about the center. Then it would be <imath>100\pi + 60\pi + 80\pi = 240\pi</imath>, following a similar logic shown in Problem 1. But now we can realize that we want to find the center of the ball. Now imagine in your head what it would look like if this happened. The first and third semi-circles would be reduced when comparing, while the second circle is the only one that gets enlarged. And since we know that the circle has a common radius and doesn't get any smaller, it is safe to say that the answer must be less than <imath>240\pi</imath>, therefore the answer is <imath>240\pi-2\pi=\boxed{\textbf{(A)}\ 238\pi}</imath>.

~apmathematician

